### otel-shop — a checkout, end to end
#
# Open in VS Code with the REST Client extension and send these in order.
# Auth lands in an httpOnly cookie, which the extension keeps for you.
#
# Everything goes through the gateway. Nothing else is published.

@host = http://localhost:3000


### 1. Sign up
# The password rules are strict: 8+ chars, one lowercase, one uppercase, one
# symbol. The phone must be a valid international number.
# You should see a trace show up the spans across 2 services (gateway and auth)
POST {{host}}/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "reader@example.com",
  "password": "Observe!123",
  "firstName": "Ada",
  "lastName": "Lovelace",
  "phone": "+2348012345678"
}


### 2. Log in
# Sets `access_token` as an httpOnly cookie. Every request below relies on it.
# You should see a trace that spans across 2 services (gateway and auth)
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "reader@example.com",
  "password": "Observe!123"
}


### 3. Browse the catalogue
# Public, no auth. 20 per page, with a `cursor` holding the last id when there
# is more to fetch.
# You should see a trace that spans across 2 services (gateway and products)
# @name catalogue
GET {{host}}/products


### 4. Next page
# Takes the cursor straight from the response above rather than hardcoding it.
# Same as above
GET {{host}}/products?cursor={{catalogue.response.body.$.cursor}}


### 5. One product
# Note the price: minor units, as a string. 172600 is ₦1,726.00.
# same as above
GET {{host}}/products/2


### 6. Add it to the cart
# You should see a trace that spans across 3 services (gateway, cart and products)
# Since this is also a protected endpoint, you may also see auth appear sometimes
POST {{host}}/cart/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": 2,
  "quantity": 3
}


### 7. See the cart
# Returns the items as a bare array, newest page first with an optional cursor.
# You should see a trace that spans across 2 services (gateway and cart)
# You could also see auth appear sometimes
GET {{host}}/cart


### 8. Change a quantity
# Replaces the quantity
# You should see a trace that spans across 2 services (gateway and cart)
# You could also see auth appear sometimes
PATCH {{host}}/cart/items/2
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quantity": 5
}


### 9. Check out
# No body. The `x-idempotency-key` header is required: send the same key twice
# and you get the same payment back rather than a second charge.
# The payment comes back `pending`. The provider reports the outcome to our
# webhook a moment later, and that is what settles the stock and clears the cart.
# Tune it with INITIALIZE_ACCEPT_RATE, WEBHOOK_SUCCESS_RATE,
# INITIALIZE_RESPONSE_DELAY_MS and CALLBACK_DELAY_MS on the payment provider.
#
# This request traces across gateway, products, cart and payments. Auth shows up
# too whenever the gateway's jwks cache hs expired.
#
# The webhook that follows is its own trace, and it does NOT include the gateway:
# the provider posts straight to payment on the compose network, which is exactly
# why that endpoint verifies an HMAC signature of its own.
#
# There are three outcomes, and each has a different shape:
#  - declined up front (INITIALIZE_ACCEPT_RATE=0) — no webhook at all. this request
#    itself returns 503, and payment releases the stock inside it. nothing on kafka
#  - accepted, then failed (WEBHOOK_SUCCESS_RATE=0) — 201 here, then a webhook trace
#    across payments and products (by kafka, to release the stock)
#  - succeeded — 201 here, then a webhook trace across payments, products (by
#    kafka, to settle the stock) and cart (by kafka, to clear the users cart)
POST {{host}}/payments/checkout
x-idempotency-key: reader-checkout-1


### 10. Payment history
# Poll this after a moment and the status will have moved to `succeeded` or
# `failed`. 
# It should span across 2 services (gateway and payments). 
# auth also, if the jwk cache has expired
GET {{host}}/payments


### 11. Confirm the cart was cleared
# Empty array on success. A failed payment leaves the cart alone so you can
# retry it.

GET {{host}}/cart


### Replay an idempotency key
# Same key as step 9. Returns the original payment rather than charging again,
# so run this before the out of stock pair below changes what is in the cart.

POST {{host}}/payments/checkout
x-idempotency-key: reader-checkout-1


### Out of stock, part 1: add it
# Products 1, 11, 21 and so on are seeded with quantity 0. Adding one still
# succeeds with a 201, the cart only asks products for the name, price and
# currency, and never checks availability. Stock is not consulted until
# checkout tries to reserve it.

POST {{host}}/cart/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": 11,
  "quantity": 1
}


### Out of stock, part 2: fail the checkout
# This is where it breaks: reserve runs `WHERE quantity >= ?`, matches nothing,
# and the checkout comes back 422. A new idempotency key, so it is a fresh
# attempt rather than a replay of step 9.

POST {{host}}/payments/checkout
x-idempotency-key: reader-checkout-oos


### Log out
# Revokes the token by `jti` in Redis, so the gateway rejects it even though it
# has not expired.

POST {{host}}/auth/logout


### Unauthorised
# After logging out, this is a 401.

GET {{host}}/cart
